    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. basically i'm worried about the \"landside\" thing you mentioned. if they have to leave the terminal to collect bags or switch flights, that's when things get messy. can you expand on that? specifically, what are the red flags that a \"transit\" is actually going to count as an \"entry\" and require a full visa? i just don't want them getting caught in some bureaucratic loophole at the border.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"landside\" situation is indeed where the bureaucratic snags appear, as it means you are officially crossing a border, even if only for a few hours. A transit that becomes an entry is usually flagged when your itinerary forces you to leave the sterile international transit area—this is when you have to collect checked luggage, clear immigration, and then re-check it for the next leg.\n\nLook out for these red flags that signal a potential \"entry\" requirement instead of a simple \"transit\":\n\n1.  **Baggage Re-check Required:** If the airline physically forces you to collect your bags at the destination airport before proceeding to the next flight, you are officially entering the country.\n2.  **Change of Terminal/Airline:** If the connecting flight is on a different airline or operates out of a completely separate terminal, you will almost certainly need to pass through immigration checkpoints.\n3.  **Duration of Stop:** While not a hard rule, very long layovers (say, over 12 hours) often encourage or necessitate leaving the airport to rest or conduct other necessary activities, pushing you into entry territory.\n\nIf you are unsure, assume you need the entry visa, as it is always safer to",
      "source": "target",
